            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">The face of this postcard reproduces a painting, captioned on the reverse, “TRIESTE – Piazza della Borsa.” The artist is Richard Carniel (1868-1915), an Italian irredentist, born in Trieste. Committed to recovering former Italian territory from the Austro-Hungarian empire, he enlisted in the Italian Legion in 1914 to fight alongside the French, and died on the battlefield. He had lived in Paris and exhibited at the Salon des Indépendants, where a retrospective of his work was held in 1920, containing landscapes, views of Paris, nudes, portraits and a self-portrait (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Benezit Dictionary of Artists</name>, Oxford Art Online). The fact that JJ was able to acquire and send through the mails an image created by an Italian irredentist is one measure of the changes that had taken place since the end of the war, when Italy annexed the city of Trieste as a result of treaties. Piazza della Borsa, named for Trieste’s old stock exchange, later became the seat of the Chamber of Commerce. Eloquently visible in this painting is the tall column in the piazza supporting the bronze figure of Leopold I (1640-1705), emperor of Austria, and, suspended from a building adjacent to the column, an Italian flag – a prohibited act in the days of Austro-Hungarian rule.</note>
            </div>
